{
  "_comment": "Declarative UI-screen configuration shared across implementations. This describes screen chrome (headers, menus, difficulty rows) so the pygame (Python) and Three.js (TypeScript) front-ends can be kept in sync from one source instead of hand-matching layouts. Board geometry lives elsewhere (see docs/plans/typescript-rewrite-same-repo.md); this file is UI only. Schema: web/src/config/screens.ts.",
  "version": 2,
  "_themeComment": "Chrome palettes, ported from the pygame THEMES registry (minesweeper/gui.py) so both front-ends offer the same looks; tests/test_theme_sync.py guards the two against drift. Field names follow the web's CSS custom properties, which do NOT map one-to-one onto the pygame keys: `panel` is the card fill (pygame `button`) and `counterBg` is the dark LED box (pygame `panel`). A colour may carry an alpha suffix (#rrggbbaa) where the web look needs one. `dark` is web-only — all six pygame presets are light.",
  "defaultTheme": "ios",
  "themes": {
    "ios": {
      "label": "Minimal iOS",
      "background": "#f2f2f7",
      "panel": "#ffffff",
      "text": "#1c1c1e",
      "muted": "#8e8e93",
      "accent": "#0a84ff",
      "onAccent": "#ffffff",
      "selected": "#ddeaff",
      "border": "#dfe0e5",
      "danger": "#ff5c5c",
      "counterBg": "#1c1c1e",
      "radius": 14,
      "shadow": "0 2px 6px rgba(60, 62, 74, 0.14)"
    },
